{
    "m_SGVersion": 0,
    "m_Type": "UnityEditor.ShaderGraph.StickyNoteData",
    "m_ObjectId": "d36937a469684a128fd3e588346659e3",
    "m_Title": "Why does this produce an isometric projection",
    "m_Content": "The shader tests the square after transforming coordinates,\nso the visible shape is the square under the inverse transform.\nT⁻¹ of a non‑uniform scale is a skew → skew = rhombus.\nImaging you have a square: (0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (0,1)\nwe are doing scale(1,0.5) then rotate(45)\nSo our coordinate world space is defind as [1,0][0, 0.5] * 1/sqrt(2)[1, -1][ 1, 1]\nAfter the coordinate is transformed, we evaluate what pixel is within the square boundary (0,0), (1,0), (1,1), (0,1)\nAfter this, we got a bunch of point, but these point is in the transformed space, we need to inverse them to get the world display space\nT-1 =sqrt(2)[0.5, 0.5][-1,1]\nThen this T-1 is applies to the point found\n(0,0) -> (0,0)\n(1,0) -> (0.7071, -1.4142)\n(1,1) -> (1,4142, 0)\n(0,1) -> (0.7071, 1.4142)\n\nAKA rotationg is done before scalling ",
    "m_TextSize": 0,
    "m_Theme": 0,
    "m_Position": {
        "serializedVersion": "2",
        "x": -1580.0,
        "y": -586.0,
        "width": 714.0,
        "height": 439.0
    },
    "m_Group": {
        "m_Id": ""
    }
}

